<binaryimage arch="MIPS:BE:32:default:default">
<!--
    kuna option bejoin (default on): MIPS o32 big-endian returns a long long in
    $2:$3 with the HIGH word in $2, the first return register.  Return
    recovery joined $2 as the LOW word, so wide_mul printed its halves swapped:
        wide_mul:  return CONCAT44((int4)((int8)a0 * a1),(int4)(... >> 0x20));   (bug)
    A function returning one int can leave something else in $3.  clang -O0
    materializes a zero in both $2 and $3 and then overwrites $2, so realeof
    and both leave a literal zero in $3 that nothing reads.  Joined in the
    ABI's order that zero became the low word and the result the high word
    (both printed return (uint8)v1 << 0x20).  A literal zero in the second
    register proves nothing either way, so such a pair keeps the old join,
    which narrows to the bool in $2.  (u64)x << 32 compiles to the same shape
    and still prints as its high word x, as it did before.
    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype), option bejoin at its
    shipped default (on).  Little-endian targets are byte-identical; the
    off pass and the trade-off the option makes are in kuna-bejoin.xml.
    Source: bejoin_mips32_be.o (mipsel-linux-gnu-gcc 10.3 -EB -O2) at 0x10000,
    bejoin_zero_mips32_O0.o (clang 14 -O0, mips-linux-gnu) at 0x20000:
        long long wide_mul(int a, int b) { return (long long)a * b; }
          mult $4,$5 / mflo $3 / jr $31 / mfhi $2 (in the delay slot)
        int realeof(unsigned a) { return a != 0 && a != 1; }
        int both(int a, int b) { return a && b; }
          addiu $2,$zero,0 / addiu $3,$zero,0 / ... / andi $2,$1,1 / jr $31
    Assert #1: wide_mul is the signed 64-bit product, mfhi (the high word) in $2.
    Assert #2: no join puts the low product first.
    Assert #3: realeof and both return the bool in $2.
    Assert #4: nothing is shifted into the high word.
-->
